title 02 | Before HTTPS: network readiness and name resolution [overview stages 1-2]
participant "Client HTTP\napplication" as C #DBEAFE
participant "OS / network\nconfiguration + resolver" as O #DBEAFE
participant "DHCP\nserver" as H #DBEAFE
participant "Recursive\nDNS resolver" as D #DBEAFE
participant "Root DNS\nserver" as R #DBEAFE
participant ".com TLD\nDNS server" as T #DBEAFE
participant "Authoritative DNS\nfor example.com" as A #DBEAFE

note over C,A
  READ FIRST: setup is conditional; DNS is its own network conversation.
  DNS returns addresses. The HTTPS request later uses a different connection.
  All shown network exchanges descend through transport, IP, link, physical.
end note

== 1a | Establish the access link, if necessary ==
O -> O: Ethernet: link negotiation / link up\nWi-Fi: discover AP, authenticate/associate;\ncomplete link security exchange when required
note over O,H #DCFCE7
  Link readiness comes before ordinary IP traffic.
  A combined home box can act as AP, switch, router, DHCP server, and NAT.
  These are separate functions even when housed in the same device.
end note

== 1b | Obtain IPv4 configuration, if absent ==
opt No usable static / leased configuration
  O -> H: DHCPDISCOVER: initial broadcast\nUDP 68 -> 67; 0.0.0.0 -> 255.255.255.255
  H --> O: DHCPOFFER: offered address + options\nDelivery may be broadcast or unicast
  O -> H: DHCPREQUEST: broadcast chosen offer
  H --> O: DHCPACK: lease + configuration options
  O -> O: Configure 192.168.1.10/24\ngateway 192.168.1.1; resolver address; lease timers
end
note over O,H #FFEDD5
  Initial broadcasts stay local unless a DHCP relay forwards them.
  DHCP renewals differ from this initial four-message example.
  A host with valid configuration skips this stage.
end note

== 2a | Check local name sources and caches ==
C -> C: Parse URL: scheme https, host www.example.com,\nport 443, path /hello
C -> O: Resolve hostname (getaddrinfo-like API)
O -> O: Check configured name sources / local DNS cache
alt Usable local result exists
  O --> C: Return cached address(es)
else Local DNS lookup needed
  note over O,D #DCFCE7
    To reach an off-subnet resolver, use a route and the gateway's MAC.
    ARP resolves the next hop if its cache entry is missing [see 04].
    Never ARP across the Internet for the DNS server's remote MAC.
  end note
  O -> D: Recursive query: www.example.com, type A\nOrdinary DNS: UDP ephemeral port -> 53
  alt Resolver has a usable cached answer
    D -> D: Use cached answer and remaining TTL
  else Answer / necessary delegations are not cached
    D -> R: Iterative query for www.example.com
    R --> D: Referral: .com nameservers\n+ address glue where needed
    D -> T: Iterative query for www.example.com
    T --> D: Referral: example.com nameservers\n+ address glue where needed
    D -> A: Query authoritative server for A record
    A --> D: Answer + record TTL\nIllustrative A = 203.0.113.20
    D -> D: Cache answer and usable referrals
  end
  D --> O: DNS response: address + remaining TTL
  O --> C: Address candidates for a connection attempt
end

== 2b | Practical alternatives and failure branches ==
note over D,A
  A recursive resolver performs the upstream work in this example.
  Cached delegations can skip root/TLD queries; CNAME aliases require follow-up.
  Authoritative NXDOMAIN means the name does not exist; it may be cached.
  Timeout / SERVFAIL is a different failure and can cause retry or fallback.
end note
note over O,D #EDE9FE
  AAAA queries obtain IPv6 candidates; A and AAAA can be requested in parallel.
  Traditional DNS also supports TCP 53; truncation can trigger TCP retry.
  DoT: DNS over TLS, typically TCP 853.
  DoH: DNS messages carried in HTTPS, typically port 443.
  A browser may use its own resolver / DoH rather than this OS path.
  Encrypted resolver access still needs a bootstrap address / configuration.
end note
note over C,D #FCE7F3
  A DNS answer does not establish HTTPS server identity.
  TLS later checks the certificate against www.example.com [03].
  DNSSEC can authenticate DNS data; it does not encrypt ordinary DNS traffic.
end note

C -> C: Select an address; start socket / connection setup [03]
legend left
  |= Color |= Role |
  |<#DBEAFE> Application | HTTP, DNS, application logic |
  |<#FCE7F3> Security | TLS above TCP; SSL is the historical name |
  |<#EDE9FE> Transport | TCP; sockets are a local API boundary |
  |<#FFEDD5> Network | IP addressing and router forwarding |
  |<#DCFCE7> Link | Ethernet / Wi-Fi framing and local delivery |
  |<#FEF3C7> Physical | Electrical, optical, or radio signals |
  Solid arrows: actions / exchanges. Dashed arrows: replies / completions.
  Arrow style does not imply a different delivery guarantee.
endlegend

@enduml
